# An ITERATOR BINDS TO WHAT IT WALKS, the way a view binds to what it views, and
# a cursor over nothing has nothing to yield. The emitter writes a cursor struct
# per `.iterate` SITE rather than per type, so both spellings below used to
# reach the C naming a struct that was never emitted -- `zc` exited 0 and gcc
# reported an unknown type name, which is the silent acceptance the project
# forbids.
#
# BOTH SPELLINGS, because they take different routes to the same construction:
# the bare name of an alias goes through the bare-type check, the instantiation
# written out goes through the generic-construction leg, and only the first was
# ever asked.
#
# The test is iteration's own, which is structural: any class with a `.call`
# method returning the appropriate option wrapper is iterable, and nothing
# declares a conformance to say so. It replaced a list of three names that
# covered only the non-generic cursors.
It: (ListIter String)

main: function is {
    a: It
    b: (ListIter String)
    c: CpIter
    print "unreached"
}
